#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fefdc81b-b940-5346-8434-9bb289e3df35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323371} {:answer/id #uuid "a85a4d02-7eb5-575e-b06e-f8e362ea6849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323372} {:answer/id #uuid "6405ecbc-222a-575f-93ac-06c22108490d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323373} {:answer/id #uuid "33e380ca-0c8d-5ab7-96da-a6873393bc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323374} {:answer/id #uuid "3562ae33-e733-5f05-95c6-3021c2597542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fefdc81b-b940-5346-8434-9bb289e3df35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323371} :answer-field/id #uuid "84b4ac1d-2e3f-5ec8-8f4b-c490be8bf844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72fe9af3-c2e0-5976-bfcc-a799e0eb9da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-170672" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceb1fd12-520c-524b-b4eb-036ae852b780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323378} {:answer/id #uuid "faa41d7b-47a0-59cd-87bf-b5da7b31df9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+s}(s+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323379} {:answer/id #uuid "6330caa9-b759-5795-9b4f-8e5074f0a48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{t+s}(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323380} {:answer/id #uuid "6346cf87-c2aa-5b83-a56f-bf202aac93d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{t+s}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323381} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc896cc-5dd8-5e11-b2e5-84684d6fcf6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceb1fd12-520c-524b-b4eb-036ae852b780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323378} :answer-field/id #uuid "f844cd4b-5331-5e19-acfc-7ca2fa0e2a25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c416fc6f-25fb-59a7-937c-9a9140fd2668" :question/math-academy-id "q-170673" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ where $x=t+s$ and $y=ts.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(ts{e}^{t+s})(1)+({e}^{t+s})(t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t+s}(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50874557-761c-542a-ae7e-8d956c467a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323385} {:answer/id #uuid "505e8226-e2f6-5494-b361-e53519f9a96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323386} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7a1f63-e1a9-5879-b67f-fe02ad41c0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323387} {:answer/id #uuid "16384e39-c067-5837-90a2-fe09b9ee183a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323388} {:answer/id #uuid "8874d854-f4f4-5d45-9b20-deeee9d70c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50874557-761c-542a-ae7e-8d956c467a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323385} :answer-field/id #uuid "368fec46-9bc5-56bd-8cf9-d520b3417b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0285d27-5a91-54ee-863f-f3912e6a5c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-170675" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}$ and $z={t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{{c}_{1}{s}^{2}+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y+z}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}\\cdot (1)+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{-({s}^{2}+{t}^{2})+2t(s+t)}{(s+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2ts-{s}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=-1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=-1+2+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7278562-24c4-5c8d-b137-b72cf3f29957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323399} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dfbbc7-bb61-55dd-bae2-097cff955fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{3}{t}^{2}-4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323400} {:answer/id #uuid "fd57f627-5212-5098-ad9a-019cfc7c020e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-5{s}^{2}+2{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323401} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b42d5a-7a9f-523f-bfa1-7240668a08fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{5}{t}^{2}+2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323402} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5608fb-6924-5a3f-806a-c9c01ddbc750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5608fb-6924-5a3f-806a-c9c01ddbc750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323403} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd427bf-cedd-5bc9-a4b3-5843d5f7aba3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "200fb821-d757-5a5f-b920-7e5ae54a1706" :question/math-academy-id "q-37138" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2y,$ where $x=s{t}^{2}$ and $y={s}^{3}-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =({s}^{3}-t)^{2}={s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(s{t}^{2})({s}^{3}-t)-2=2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =({s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2})({t}^{2})+(2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2)(3{s}^{2}) \\\\  & ={s}^{6}{t}^{2}-2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}+6{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2} \\\\  & =7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac48423-b1a6-5729-9305-5a0b35417b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165044} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3b5b84-e8e3-5a19-9667-992ceeaa8a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "se^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165045} {:answer/id #uuid "08a5bbc3-66f9-58d6-978b-b890eafaf65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165046} {:answer/id #uuid "78ffae4a-1094-5f42-82ea-fc7cb5423b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ste^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165047} {:answer/id #uuid "7938068f-a64c-5fa7-959c-06d79d4adefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "se^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce3b5b84-e8e3-5a19-9667-992ceeaa8a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "se^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165045} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ea9526-0f30-56be-a9c1-49879378e893" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a13d6706-d1e1-54bc-9c0b-137475532529" :question/math-academy-id "q-37205" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2x+xy,$ where $x=s{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2+t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =s{e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2+t{e}^{s})(0)+(s{e}^{s})({e}^{s}) \\\\  & =s{e}^{2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67da27f-16cc-5cd7-85c0-0518fdb57641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{3}\\cos (2x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165140} {:answer/id #uuid "e19e73c6-32ab-5426-9cd7-e7fea7f1a50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\cos (2x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165141} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3c3df-8ea5-5d47-b7b9-986a4b9b51b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x + 1)\\sin (x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165142} {:answer/id #uuid "1f359472-0e15-5b55-8ac1-bde2ddee935c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sin^{2} (2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165143} {:answer/id #uuid "52db7be6-7558-5ec9-9e78-69737f7ea583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^{2} + 1)\\cos (x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e19e73c6-32ab-5426-9cd7-e7fea7f1a50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\cos (2x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165141} :answer-field/id #uuid "779c9969-3fec-5f93-9a07-8cff13fe7fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ec1810-6288-5fe9-a1ed-f6d4d3a585e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94588" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = \\sin (x^{2} + y)$ and $y = x^{2}$, compute $\\frac{df}{dx}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Since $y=x^2$, the total derivative is $df/dx=f_x+f_y\\,dy/dx$. Here $f_x=2x\\cos(x^2+y)$, $f_y=\\cos(x^2+y)$, and $dy/dx=2x$. Thus $$\\frac{df}{dx}=2x\\cos(2x^2)+2x\\cos(2x^2)=4x\\cos(2x^2).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d89b8cf-6caf-5298-9440-92429d6ddf4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(t+s){e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323413} {:answer/id #uuid "fa889f70-284b-55c3-988d-184cc294d6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(t-s){e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323414} {:answer/id #uuid "bf59e1e7-0b31-57f2-88b3-f63cf40d53b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323415} {:answer/id #uuid "dd78109e-8abd-5f23-940a-2c93f303b365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(s-1){e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323416} {:answer/id #uuid "46ae10af-1def-5371-9dcb-55bc31c10af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t(s+1){e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf59e1e7-0b31-57f2-88b3-f63cf40d53b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323415} :answer-field/id #uuid "b498291b-53fd-50bf-af45-63cad4c51b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c71a1fc7-14f8-5f45-8aa5-6faf468933ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-94812" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz,$ where $x=2,$ $y=s{e}^{t}$ and $z=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=xz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2s{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =s{e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(ts{e}^{t+s})\\cdot 0+(2t{e}^{s})\\cdot s{e}^{t}+(2s{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{s} \\\\  & =2ts{e}^{t+s}+2s{e}^{t+s} \\\\  & =2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "916f71d9-1461-5cbc-a96a-fa2facdfc84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t + 4s - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165152} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf9c459-9ead-5506-8296-67e11bcaa57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t - 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165153} {:answer/id #uuid "cff4bcaf-76ab-55eb-8c51-2853168f6c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165154} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6ff427-9bf3-58ed-9f8f-8edd6d37b239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - 8s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165155} {:answer/id #uuid "5998c176-c992-5217-8977-1683ab72fccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5998c176-c992-5217-8977-1683ab72fccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165156} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ab6d1c-5493-5461-9355-8385cc0e3f0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db13dec0-b66e-50d3-9ee6-bdc2288dd174" :question/math-academy-id "q-94814" :question/problem "Let $f(x, y, z) = 4x + 3y - 2z$, where $x = 2s^{2} - t$, $y = s + 3t^{2}$ and $z = 4s^{2} + t^{2}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The partial derivatives of $f$ are $f_x=4$, $f_y=3$, $f_z=-2$. Holding $t$ fixed gives $x_s=4s$, $y_s=1$, and $z_s=8s$. Therefore $$f_s=4(4s)+3(1)-2(8s)=3.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a631c9b1-f15a-567c-8779-26da12a59286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "st({s}^{2}{t}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323427} {:answer/id #uuid "371e683f-4245-5dc5-bc94-f0b1e611b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323428} {:answer/id #uuid "af0ddd68-d47f-57cc-b0eb-868da8ab44fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323429} {:answer/id #uuid "5f471692-18b5-5e28-80d9-f3cafbcddd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}-{s}^{2}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323430} {:answer/id #uuid "f3115d56-c996-52a9-9fa4-83a40f1f3531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{st+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af0ddd68-d47f-57cc-b0eb-868da8ab44fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323429} :answer-field/id #uuid "e52e23f4-47d9-5bf1-b5e3-59f36d43abb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f54cad8f-6f81-5bf1-9373-fb4d7af3562f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94816" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2{e}^{y}+4z,$ where $x=t{s}^{2},$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})$ and $z={s}^{2}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})}=-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2ts, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}})\\cdot 2ts+(-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}))\\cdot (\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}})+(4)\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-4s+8s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}+4s \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca1bdaf6-5fbd-5ebd-8385-88367c25d304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s+{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323455} {:answer/id #uuid "6251e754-9fc0-5e9b-9fbc-1d45bbbd5cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323456} {:answer/id #uuid "ade39907-996f-5f76-b1d9-298e6d3b4f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323457} {:answer/id #uuid "8d879a89-1789-53e5-be9a-50cdc59af0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323458} {:answer/id #uuid "7a87e8e4-ced1-5b8f-89d5-4804d4e56929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6251e754-9fc0-5e9b-9fbc-1d45bbbd5cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323456} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcd6dbb-dde2-5e3e-b7e9-76d3d882377d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a712ea0-8d4b-5b2f-8c02-2dea8af5517a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95084" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s)+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{cot}^{2}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36d55b9-593a-5ec9-87bd-77a19405c36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323462} {:answer/id #uuid "19bea0db-5dd8-5ee3-af2f-2d087dfa025c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-x}}{1+x{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323463} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf3228e-0230-53d4-84f2-0a37a15d3d84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323464} {:answer/id #uuid "0c064251-3a4f-5aba-9678-eb91786b561a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{1+x{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323465} {:answer/id #uuid "1b35a2a4-6b46-5313-8253-a82a89030889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b35a2a4-6b46-5313-8253-a82a89030889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323466} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d616879-e133-50c3-a2d8-3aaa7795fdcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f15b5a69-260b-56fe-be10-0c6581bada1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95088" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}$ and $y={e}^{-x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}\\cdot (-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6b2e565-cfe8-5138-9494-c6beec220fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1 + 2x}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165170} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a8779d-9dc3-5a97-b6f3-3f6be1bd2ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (1 + x) - \\frac{1 + 2x^{2}}{1 + x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165171} {:answer/id #uuid "05885086-515f-58da-892a-3266e6813c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (x + x^{2}) + \\frac{1 + 2x}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165172} {:answer/id #uuid "412ab7e8-5ebd-54ae-b183-23608f50725f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}\\ln (1 + x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165173} {:answer/id #uuid "73a04f2c-4fe2-59f8-973b-201032c01729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (1 + x^{2}) - \\frac{1 + 3x^{2}}{1 + 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05885086-515f-58da-892a-3266e6813c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (x + x^{2}) + \\frac{1 + 2x}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165172} :answer-field/id #uuid "c474d49f-63e2-5a40-bad2-2872ce44e607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56630566-56de-5b10-a1f2-1989c1203d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-95121" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = x\\ln (x + y)$ and $y = x^{2}$, compute $\\frac{df}{dx}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The partial derivatives are $f_x=\\ln(x+y)+x/(x+y)$ and $f_y=x/(x+y)$. Since $dy/dx=2x$, $$\\frac{df}{dx}=\\ln(x+x^2)+\\frac{x+2x^2}{x+x^2}=\\ln(x+x^2)+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x},$$ on the domain $x+x^2>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "480dbbcb-0023-5725-897f-7aca3cb37afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}(x-6)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323483} {:answer/id #uuid "ff891614-1ae5-51de-9f7b-3bb0461cf582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(4x-3)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323484} {:answer/id #uuid "f34fb796-c3ab-5386-b050-c225cc8aa252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323485} {:answer/id #uuid "efb9c247-d27a-5b67-9844-9f880c46afaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(3x+4)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323486} {:answer/id #uuid "154a97e3-ea69-5ad4-883b-a6637f10fc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "154a97e3-ea69-5ad4-883b-a6637f10fc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323487} :answer-field/id #uuid "af3b29e2-aa0e-5c69-92fb-7158535efc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38ad2292-5f69-5e15-93f2-ebba34819eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95122" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{(x+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+1)-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
